210-### 1155 — A contract can name its consumers correctly and still be scheduled before all of them (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r9)
211-- FINDING: **no reviewer verdict asked for this.** r8 was handed off to review; r9 is a self-audit of the interval. r8 was re-run untouched first and reproduced every gate (1331 checks, 164/164, 44 board checks, exit 0), so nothing in r9 repairs drift that had already appeared.
212:- ROOT CAUSE: the matrix has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1 and it was **never compared against the board's dependency graph**. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: 19 declared consumers, **16 reachable, 3 not** — `TASK-260715-1o9wjz`, `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`, `TASK-260715-29ws8l`. With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run all three while the matrix sat in `analysis` under its **eighth** changes-requested verdict, authoring the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract against an unaccepted contract.
213-- FINDING: this is the **verdict-05 F2 class one field over**. Verdict 05 checked what a consumer *says*; rules A1/P1 check which *revision* it names; nothing checked *when it runs*. A1/P1 only exist for the two consumers carrying a revision label — every other consumer has no label, so the dependency edge is the sole ordering mechanism, and **a missing edge fails silently because nothing reads a prose list.**
214-- FINDING: four more elements carrying obligations were absent from `consumers` entirely, three assigned from inside **prose fields** — `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` named `TASK-260715-35nc5m` for eight revisions, two sentences named `TASK-260717-xempiv` for five. Hence D1's derived set scans **every string** in the contract, not three named lists.
215-- FIX: one board edge — `link(TASK-260715-29ws8l, blocked_by=TASK-260715-ypo7yo)`, cycle-checked over the whole graph first. It orders all three because 29ws8l already blocks the other two, and it is semantically real: 29ws8l **AC2** specifies "App Group and `providerConfiguration` data" and **AC4** specifies "Keychain accessibility, access group" — exactly what rules G4 and K2 decide. Authoring it without this matrix reproduces the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.
