302-- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
303-- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
304-- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
305:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
306-- FINDING: One property macOS **cannot** offer that iOS does: the System keychain unlocks at boot from root-only `/private/var/db/SystemKey`, so the secret is **not** protected by the user's login password. ~~(2) deletion cannot be synchronous — the user-context app cannot delete a root-owned item while the provider is stopped, so revocation is a provider request reconciled by a start-time sweep.~~ **CORRECTED in r2 — see the 0851 entry.** Revocation *is* synchronous whenever the NE configuration is installed and enabled; the r1 claim rested on a misread SDK header.
307-- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
308-- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
